WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:04.880
A circuit has to be built.

2
00:00:05.240 --> 00:00:06.760
It also has to be kept running.

3
00:00:07.620 --> 00:00:10.400
Picture a circuit that was built without one key protein.

4
00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:13.100
Now switch that protein on in an adult.

5
00:00:13.720 --> 00:00:14.860
Can that undo the damage?

6
00:00:15.360 --> 00:00:18.240
If the wiring was built wrong for good, it stays wrong.

7
00:00:18.900 --> 00:00:23.080
If the wiring is sound but needs the protein to keep working, the circuit should

8
00:00:23.080 --> 00:00:24.080
recover.

9
00:00:24.533 --> 00:00:26.953
Rett syndrome puts this question to the test.

10
00:00:27.813 --> 00:00:30.653
Girls with Rett syndrome seem to develop typically for many months.

11
00:00:31.173 --> 00:00:33.533
Then they lose purposeful hand use and speech.

12
00:00:34.293 --> 00:00:38.373
Most cases come from mutations in a gene called MECP2.

13
00:00:38.993 --> 00:00:43.793
The gene makes a protein that binds DNA where the cytosines carry methyl groups.

14
00:00:44.817 --> 00:00:46.757
Now look at mice that lack this gene.

15
00:00:47.397 --> 00:00:48.957
They appear typical for several weeks.

16
00:00:49.477 --> 00:00:53.017
Then they develop tremor, breathing changes and reduced movement.

17
00:00:53.757 --> 00:00:55.877
Their neurons do not die in large numbers.

18
00:00:56.537 --> 00:00:59.357
Instead the neurons are smaller, with simpler dendrites.

19
00:01:00.450 --> 00:01:05.430
In two thousand seven, Jacky Guy, Adrian Bird and colleagues built mice in which the

20
00:01:05.430 --> 00:01:08.890
gene is silent. A blocking sequence sits inside it.

21
00:01:09.250 --> 00:01:11.590
The mice also carry an enzyme called Cre.

22
00:01:12.250 --> 00:01:16.370
Cre enters the nucleus only after the mouse receives the drug tamoxifen.

23
00:01:16.950 --> 00:01:20.110
Then Cre removes the block, and the gene comes back on.

24
00:01:20.110 --> 00:01:22.670
So the team chooses when the gene returns.

25
00:01:23.800 --> 00:01:25.260
This is the key test.

26
00:01:25.860 --> 00:01:28.400
The team treated adult mice that already had symptoms.

27
00:01:29.160 --> 00:01:32.480
They gave tamoxifen, which restored MeCP2.

28
00:01:33.080 --> 00:01:36.960
Many symptoms reversed, in young adult mice and in older adult mice.

29
00:01:37.580 --> 00:01:40.640
In mice without the silenced gene, tamoxifen did nothing.

30
00:01:41.733 --> 00:01:44.973
So in these mice, the circuits were not permanently miswired.

31
00:01:45.653 --> 00:01:49.293
They formed without MeCP2, and worked again when it returned.

32
00:01:50.033 --> 00:01:54.053
Deleting it in adult mice also causes symptoms, so it is needed continuously.

33
00:01:54.833 --> 00:01:55.873
This is a mouse result.

34
00:01:56.373 --> 00:01:58.773
Whether it holds for an older person is not known.

35
00:01:59.313 --> 00:02:03.013
Too much MeCP2 is also harmful, so the dose matters.

36
00:02:03.593 --> 00:02:07.073
Circuits need some proteins to keep running, not only to be built.
